\title[The divisibility-covering frontier]{The Divisibility-Covering Frontier:\\ A Sandwich Theorem Around the Umans--Wang Target $(1/3,1/3)$}
\author{Aristotle (Harmonic)}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A pair of finite sets $S,T\subset\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$ is a \emph{divisibility covering} of $[n]=\{1,\dots,n\}$ if every $i\in[n]$ divides some positive difference $s-t$ with $s\in S$, $t\in T$, $t<s$. Umans and Wang have shown that coverings with $|S|,|T|\le n^{\beta}$ and elements at most $\exp(n^{\alpha})$, at the symmetric point $(\alpha,\beta)=(1/3,1/3)$, would improve the exponent of deterministic integer factoring from $1/5$ to $1/6$; they leave open whether the natural constraint $\alpha\ge 1-2\beta$ is achievable. We give a complete, elementary account of what is provable around this target. We prove (i) an explicit \emph{counting wall} $4^k\le 2k\,M^{|S||T|}$ for every covering of $[2k]$ with elements at most $M$; (ii) the resulting \emph{exponent constraint} $\alpha+\beta_S+\beta_T\ge 1$ for every asymptotically feasible triple; (iii) feasibility of the block-product covering for every $\alpha>1-\beta$ with $|T|=1$; hence (iv) the \emph{one-sided frontier is exactly} $\alpha=1-\beta$; and (v) the \emph{sandwich} at $\beta=1/3$: feasibility forces $\alpha\ge 1/3$, and every $\alpha>2/3$ is feasible. We also prove a large-prime budget $y^{\pi(n)-\pi(y)}\le M^{|S||T|}$, a resultant reformulation of the covering condition for disjoint integer sets, the necessity of the disjointness guard, and the correctness of the double-logarithmic unit $\ln\ln M/\ln n$ (the single-logarithmic reading diverges). Finally we report a negative experimental campaign---six construction families, fifteen independent adversarial reviews, zero genuine coverings at the target---and explain, via the sandwich, why the one empirical success at $(1/2,2/3)$ carries no information about the open interval $\alpha\in[1/3,2/3]$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background}
Deterministic integer factoring is far slower than its randomised counterpart. Algorithms in the Pollard--Strassen and Lehman lineage factor $N$ deterministically in time $N^{c+o(1)}$, and every improvement in $c$ is hard won. Umans and Wang \cite{UW} isolated a purely combinatorial ingredient whose existence, in their framework, controls the exponent: a \emph{divisibility covering} of an initial segment of the integers by the differences of two small sets of moderately sized numbers. In their parametrisation, coverings at $(\alpha,\beta)=(1/3,1/3)$ would yield exponent $1/6$, improving the exponent $1/5$. They record that a counting argument forces $\alpha\ge 1-2\beta$ and state that they do not know whether $\alpha=1-2\beta$ is achievable.

This paper is about the combinatorial problem alone. We do not construct a covering at $(1/3,1/3)$, and we do not refute one. We establish, with complete proofs, the exact region surrounding the target, and separate three statements that are easily conflated:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item a \emph{trivial boundary} $\alpha+\beta\le 1+o(1)$ describing where elementary constructions live;
\item the \emph{counting constraint} $\alpha\ge 1-2\beta$, which is the binding lower bound;
\item the \emph{trivial covering point} $\alpha=1-\beta+o(1)$, which block products attain.
\end{enumerate}
At $\beta=1/3$ the constraint gives $\alpha=1/3$ (with zero slack) and the trivial covering gives $\alpha=2/3$. The gap between them is precisely the open content.

\subsection{Summary of results}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Counting Wall} (Theorem~\ref{thm:wall}). If $S,T$ cover $[2k]$ ($k\ge1$) and $s\le M$ for all $s\in S$, then $4^k\le 2k\,M^{|S||T|}$.
\item \textbf{Exponent Constraint} (Theorem~\ref{thm:constraint}). Every feasible triple satisfies $\alpha+\beta_S+\beta_T\ge 1$.
\item \textbf{Trivial Covering} (Theorem~\ref{thm:trivial}). For $0<\beta\le 1$ and $\alpha>1-\beta$ the triple $(\alpha,\beta,0)$ is feasible.
\item \textbf{One-Sided Frontier} (Corollary~\ref{cor:onesided}). With $|T|\le1$ the frontier is exactly $\alpha=1-\beta$.
\item \textbf{Sandwich} (Corollary~\ref{cor:sandwich}). At $\beta=1/3$: feasibility forces $\alpha\ge\frac13$, and every $\alpha>\frac23$ is feasible.
\item \textbf{Large-Prime Budget} (Proposition~\ref{prop:largeprime}), \textbf{Resultant Bridge} (Proposition~\ref{prop:resultant}), \textbf{Guard} (Proposition~\ref{prop:guard}), and \textbf{Units} (Proposition~\ref{prop:units}).
\end{itemize}
Section~\ref{sec:exp} reports the experimental campaign.

\section{Definitions}
Throughout, $[n]=\{1,2,\dots,n\}$ and $\ln$ is the natural logarithm.

\begin{definition}[Divisibility covering]
Let $n\ge0$ and let $S,T$ be finite sets of non-negative integers. We say $(S,T)$ \emph{covers} $[n]$ if for every $i\in[n]$ there exist $s\in S$ and $t\in T$ with $t<s$ and $i\mid s-t$.
\end{definition}

The strict inequality $t<s$ is the \emph{disjointness guard}: the zero difference never counts. Covering is monotone: if $(S,T)$ covers $[m]$ and $n\le m$, it covers $[n]$.

\begin{definition}[Difference product]
The set of \emph{positive pairs} is $P(S,T)=\{(s,t)\in S\times T: t<s\}$, and the \emph{difference product} is
\[ D(S,T)=\prod_{(s,t)\in P(S,T)}(s-t). \]
It is a positive integer, and $|P(S,T)|\le|S|\,|T|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
$L_n=\lcm(1,2,\dots,n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Feasibility]
A triple of reals $(\alpha,\beta_S,\beta_T)$ is \emph{feasible} if for all sufficiently large $n$ there exist finite $S,T\subset\mathbb{Z}_{\ge0}$ with
\[ |S|\le n^{\beta_S},\qquad |T|\le n^{\beta_T},\qquad s\le \exp(n^{\alpha})\ \ (s\in S),\qquad (S,T)\text{ covers }[n]. \]
\end{definition}

Only elements of $S$ are bounded; elements of $T$ exceeding $\max S$ never occur in a positive pair, so no generality is lost. Feasibility is upward-closed: if $(\alpha,\beta_S,\beta_T)$ is feasible and $\alpha\le\alpha'$, $\beta_S\le\beta_S'$, $\beta_T\le\beta_T'$, then $(\alpha',\beta_S',\beta_T')$ is feasible.

\begin{remark}[The double-exponential unit]
Since the magnitude bound is $\exp(n^\alpha)$, the parameter $\alpha$ is double-exponential. A construction with largest element $M$ witnesses
\[ \alpha_{\mathrm{meas}}(n,M)=\frac{\ln\ln M}{\ln n}, \]
not $\ln M/\ln n$; see Proposition~\ref{prop:units}.
\end{remark}

\section{The counting wall}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:lcmdvd}
If $(S,T)$ covers $[n]$, then $L_n\mid D(S,T)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $i\in[n]$ choose $(s,t)$ with $t<s$ and $i\mid s-t$; then $(s,t)\in P(S,T)$ and $s-t$ is a factor of $D(S,T)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:binom}
For every $k\ge0$, $\binom{2k}{k}\mid L_{2k}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $k=0$ both sides are $1$. For $k\ge1$, by Legendre's formula
\[ v_p\binom{2k}{k}=\sum_{j\ge1}\left(\left\lfloor\frac{2k}{p^j}\right\rfloor-2\left\lfloor\frac{k}{p^j}\right\rfloor\right), \]
where each summand lies in $\{0,1\}$ and vanishes for $p^j>2k$. Hence $p^{v_p\binom{2k}{k}}\le 2k$, so this prime power is one of $1,\dots,2k$ and divides $L_{2k}$. As this holds for every prime $p$, the claim follows. (Equivalently, by Kummer's theorem, $v_p\binom{2k}{k}$ counts the carries in $k+k$ in base $p$.)
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:four}
For $k\ge1$, $4^k\le 2k\binom{2k}{k}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$4^k=\sum_{j=0}^{2k}\binom{2k}{j}$; group the end terms $\binom{2k}{0}+\binom{2k}{2k}=2\le\binom{2k}{k}$, leaving $2k$ groups each at most the central coefficient.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:dle}
If $M\ge1$ and $s\le M$ for all $s\in S$, then $D(S,T)\le M^{|S||T|}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each factor satisfies $0<s-t\le s\le M$, and there are at most $|S||T|$ factors.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Counting Wall]\label{thm:wall}
Let $k\ge1$. If $(S,T)$ covers $[2k]$ and every $s\in S$ satisfies $s\le M$, then
\[ 4^k\le 2k\cdot M^{|S|\,|T|}. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Covering $1$ requires some $s\in S$ with $s>t\ge0$, so $M\ge1$. By Lemmas~\ref{lem:binom} and~\ref{lem:lcmdvd}, $\binom{2k}{k}\mid L_{2k}\mid D(S,T)$, and $D(S,T)>0$, hence $\binom{2k}{k}\le D(S,T)\le M^{|S||T|}$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:dle}. Conclude with Lemma~\ref{lem:four}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Counting Wall, real form]\label{thm:realwall}
Let $n\ge2$ and suppose $(S,T)$ covers $[n]$ with $|S|\le n^{\beta_S}$, $|T|\le n^{\beta_T}$ and $s\le\exp(n^\alpha)$ for all $s\in S$. Then
\[ (n-1)\ln2\le\ln n+n^{\alpha+\beta_S+\beta_T}. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $k=\lfloor n/2\rfloor\ge1$, so $2k\le n\le 2k+1$, and $(S,T)$ covers $[2k]$. With $M=\lfloor\exp(n^\alpha)\rfloor$ every $s\in S$ satisfies $s\le M$. Theorem~\ref{thm:wall} gives $4^k\le 2k\,M^{m}\le n\exp(n^\alpha)^m$ with $m=|S||T|$; take logarithms and use $2k\ge n-1$ and $m\,n^\alpha\le n^{\alpha+\beta_S+\beta_T}$.
\end{proof}

\section{The exponent constraint}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:gap}
If $\gamma<1$ then $\ln x+x^{\gamma}<(x-1)\ln2$ for all sufficiently large real $x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
With $c=\frac14\ln 2$, for large $x$ both $\ln x\le cx$ and $x^\gamma\le cx$, so the left side is at most $\frac12x\ln2<(x-1)\ln 2$ once $x>2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponent Constraint]\label{thm:constraint}
If $\alpha+\beta_S+\beta_T<1$, then for all sufficiently large $n$ no pair $(S,T)$ with $|S|\le n^{\beta_S}$, $|T|\le n^{\beta_T}$ and $\max S\le\exp(n^\alpha)$ covers $[n]$. Consequently every feasible triple satisfies
\[ \alpha+\beta_S+\beta_T\ge1. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:realwall} with Lemma~\ref{lem:gap} at $\gamma=\alpha+\beta_S+\beta_T$.
\end{proof}

At $\beta_S=\beta_T=\beta$ this is the constraint $\alpha\ge1-2\beta$. The argument uses only that $L_n$ is exponentially large; the true size $\ln L_n\sim n$ (prime number theorem) versus the elementary $\ln L_n\ge n\ln 2-O(\ln n)$ affects constants, not exponents. At $(1/3,1/3,1/3)$ the inequality $\ln L_n\lesssim|S||T|\ln M$ reads $n\lesssim n^{1/3}\cdot n^{1/3}\cdot n^{1/3}$: the target saturates the bound.

\section{Constructions and the frontier}

For $L\ge1$ and $j\ge0$ let $B_L(j)=(jL+1)(jL+2)\cdots(jL+L)$ and
\[ S_{n,L}=\{B_L(j):0\le j<\lceil n/L\rceil\},\qquad T=\{0\}. \]

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:block}
For $L\ge1$, $(S_{n,L},\{0\})$ covers $[n]$; moreover $|S_{n,L}|\le\lceil n/L\rceil$ and every element is at most $(n+L)^L$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $i\in[n]$ put $j=\lfloor(i-1)/L\rfloor$; then $jL+1\le i\le jL+L$, $j<\lceil n/L\rceil$, and $i\mid B_L(j)-0>0$. For the size, $jL+L\le\lceil n/L\rceil L\le n+L-1$ and $B_L(j)$ has $L$ factors, each at most $jL+L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:analytic}
Let $\beta>0$ and $\alpha>1-\beta$. For all sufficiently large $x$: $x\ge1$, $x^\beta\ge2$ and $3x^{1-\beta}\ln(4x)\le x^\alpha$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
With $\delta=\alpha-(1-\beta)>0$, $\ln(4x)\le x^\delta/3$ for large $x$; multiply by $3x^{1-\beta}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Trivial Covering]\label{thm:trivial}
If $0<\beta\le1$ and $\alpha>1-\beta$, then $(\alpha,\beta,0)$ is feasible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For large $n$ take $L=\lceil2n^{1-\beta}\rceil$, so $2n^{1-\beta}\le L\le3n^{1-\beta}\le 3n$ and $n+L\le4n$; use $(S_{n,L},\{0\})$. With $B=\lceil n/L\rceil$ we have $BL<n+L\le n^\beta L$, because $n=n^{1-\beta}n^\beta\le\frac12n^\beta L$ and $L\le\frac12 n^\beta L$; thus $|S|\le B<n^\beta$. Clearly $|T|=1=n^0$. Finally each element is at most $(n+L)^L$ and $L\ln(n+L)\le3n^{1-\beta}\ln(4n)\le n^\alpha$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:analytic}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[One-Sided Frontier]\label{cor:onesided}
For $0<\beta\le1$: if $(\alpha,\beta,0)$ is feasible then $\alpha\ge1-\beta$; and if $\alpha>1-\beta$ then $(\alpha,\beta,0)$ is feasible.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:constraint} with $\beta_T=0$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:trivial}.
\end{proof}

Thus with $|T|\le1$ nothing beats block products, and \emph{any covering at $(1/3,1/3)$ must be genuinely two-sided}.

\begin{corollary}[Sandwich at $\beta=1/3$]\label{cor:sandwich}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item If $(\alpha,\frac13,\frac13)$ is feasible then $\alpha\ge\frac13$.
\item If $\alpha>\frac23$ then $(\alpha,\frac13,\frac13)$ is feasible.
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
(i) Theorem~\ref{thm:constraint}. (ii) Theorem~\ref{thm:trivial} gives $(\alpha,\frac13,0)$; enlarge $\beta_T$ by monotonicity.
\end{proof}

The open content at $\beta=1/3$ is therefore exactly $\alpha\in[\frac13,\frac23]$.

\begin{corollary}[The traded point is dominated]\label{cor:traded}
$(\frac25,\frac23,0)$ is feasible, hence so is $(\frac12,\frac23,\frac23)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$\frac25>1-\frac23$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:trivial} and monotonicity.
\end{proof}

\section{Further structure}

\begin{proposition}[Large-Prime Budget]\label{prop:largeprime}
If $(S,T)$ covers $[n]$, $M\ge1$ and $s\le M$ for all $s\in S$, then for every $y\ge0$
\[ y^{\#\{p\text{ prime}:\,y<p\le n\}}\le M^{|S|\,|T|}. \]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Each prime in $(y,n]$ divides $L_n$, hence $D(S,T)$; being pairwise coprime, their product divides $D(S,T)$. That product is at least $y^{\#}$, and $D(S,T)\le M^{|S||T|}$.
\end{proof}

With $y=n^{2/3}$ at the target, the left side has logarithm $\sim\frac23n$ and the right side at most $n$: large primes consume a constant fraction of the budget, and a typical difference must carry on the order of $n^{1/3}/\ln n$ distinct primes from $(n^{2/3},n]$.

Integer sets $S,T\subset\mathbb{Z}$ \emph{cover} $[n]$ (in the integer sense) if each $i\in[n]$ divides some $s-t$, $s\in S$, $t\in T$. Put $P_T(X)=\prod_{t\in T}(X-t)$.

\begin{proposition}[Resultant Bridge]\label{prop:resultant}
If $S,T\subset\mathbb{Z}$ are disjoint and cover $[n]$, then $R(S,T)=\prod_{s\in S}P_T(s)=\prod_{s\in S}\prod_{t\in T}(s-t)$ is a nonzero integer divisible by $L_n$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Each $i\in[n]$ divides a factor $s-t$, hence $R$; so $L_n\mid R$. Disjointness makes every factor nonzero.
\end{proof}

Up to sign $R(S,T)=\Res(P_S,P_T)$, so covering becomes a single divisibility between two integers of size $\exp(O(n))$ at the target.

\begin{proposition}[The guard is essential]\label{prop:guard}
For every $n$ and $a\in\mathbb{Z}$, $S=T=\{a\}$ covers $[n]$ in the integer sense.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$a-a=0$ is divisible by every $i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Units]\label{prop:units}
Let $n>1$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $\ln\ln\exp(n^\alpha)/\ln n=\alpha$ for every real $\alpha$.
\item If $1<M\le\exp(n^\alpha)$ then $\ln\ln M/\ln n\le\alpha$.
\item If $\alpha>0$ then $\ln\exp(x^\alpha)/\ln x=x^\alpha/\ln x\to\infty$ as $x\to\infty$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
(i) $\ln n^\alpha=\alpha\ln n$. (ii) $0<\ln M\le n^\alpha$. (iii) $\ln x=o(x^\alpha)$.
\end{proof}

For $M=\exp(n^{1/3})$ the correct reading is $1/3$, while $\ln M/\ln n\approx7.2$ at $n=10^6$ and $\approx362$ at $n=10^{12}$.

\section{Experimental campaign}\label{sec:exp}

The following is an empirical report; none of its statements is a theorem. Six construction families were implemented, each with a harness checking coverage on all of $[n]$ (composites included), enforcing $t<s$, and reporting $\alpha$ in the double-logarithmic unit: (1) multi-scale difference covers; (2) smooth-number differences; (3) prime-power/valuation structure; (4) interval and Cantor-type structured sets; (5) assembly of many primorial arithmetic progressions from the Umans--Wang framework; (6) a lower-bound attempt. Each run was examined by two independent adversarial reviewers who re-executed the harness.

All fifteen verdicts concluded that no genuine covering at the target was produced. Several initial over-claims were withdrawn, typically due to checking only primes, allowing $s=t$, or reporting $\ln M/\ln n$. A disjointness-guarded greedy search reaches full coverage at the traded point $(\alpha,\beta)=(\frac12,\frac23)$, but only $0.40$--$0.54$ at $(\frac13,\frac13)$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:traded} the traded point lies inside the region already achieved by block products, so this success is uninformative about the open interval.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Covering verification]
Given $n,S,T$, form $\Delta=\{s-t:t<s\}$ and test, for each $i\le n$, whether some $d\in\Delta$ satisfies $i\mid d$. Cost $O(n|S||T|)$ reductions. Note $L_n\mid D(S,T)$ is necessary but not sufficient.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Block-product construction]
Given $n,\beta$, set $L=\lceil2n^{1-\beta}\rceil$ and output $S=\{B_L(j):j<\lceil n/L\rceil\}$, $T=\{0\}$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Wall test]
Given $(\alpha,\beta_S,\beta_T)$ and $n$, if $(n-1)\ln2>\ln n+n^{\alpha+\beta_S+\beta_T}$ then no covering with these budgets exists at this $n$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Unit-correct exponent]
Report $\ln\ln M/\ln n$, computing $\ln M$ from the bit length of $M$.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Discussion}
The feasible region is upward-closed and lies in $\alpha+\beta_S+\beta_T\ge1$. On the face $\beta_T=0$ it is known exactly. At $\beta_S=\beta_T=\frac13$ we know only $\frac13\le\alpha_{\min}\le\frac23$. A covering at the target must make $T$ carry real weight (Corollary~\ref{cor:onesided}), waste essentially none of the budget $|S||T|\ln M$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:constraint}), pack $\gg n^{1/3}/\ln n$ large primes into a typical difference (Proposition~\ref{prop:largeprime}), and make one resultant of size $\exp(O(n))$ divisible by $L_n$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:resultant}).

The natural two-sided idea---choosing each $s$ by the Chinese remainder theorem with $s\equiv t_k\pmod{Q_k}$---has $\ln s\approx\sum_k\ln Q_k$: it pays in magnitude for every modulus served, as a block product does. Both directions of the question are significant: achievability would, by \cite{UW}, give deterministic factoring in time $N^{1/6+o(1)}$; impossibility would show that the counting constraint is not tight.

\section{Future directions}
\begin{conjecture}[Two-sided collapse for CRT coverings]
If every $s\in S$ is the CRT solution of $s\equiv t_k\pmod{Q_k}$ for a fixed list $(t_k)\subset T$ and pairwise coprime $Q_k$, then every feasible triple realised by such coverings satisfies $\alpha+\beta_S\ge1$.
\end{conjecture}
Further problems: (a) for disjoint $S,T$ with $|S|=|T|=n^{1/3}$ and entries at most $\exp(n^{1/3})$, decide whether $L_n\mid\Res(P_S,P_T)$ forces the differences to be pairwise almost coprime; (b) show that a positive proportion of differences in a target covering carry $\gg n^{1/3}/\ln n$ primes from $(n^{2/3},n]$, and study equality in Proposition~\ref{prop:largeprime}; (c) standardise experimental reporting on $\ln\ln M/\ln n$.

\section{Conclusion}
The target $(1/3,1/3)$ sits exactly on the counting wall, while block products sit on the one-sided frontier $\alpha=1-\beta$, which is sharp for one-sided coverings. The open problem is precisely $\alpha\in[\frac13,\frac23]$ at $\beta=\frac13$; it is open, and hard, in both directions.

